<title>USA: Iraq buys U.S. wheat for second week in a row-USDA.</title>
<headline>Iraq buys U.S. wheat for second week in a row-USDA.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>Iraq bought U.S. wheat for the second week in a row, purchasing 50,000 tonnes in the week ended March 27, the U.S. Agriculture Department said in its weekly export sales report.</p>
<p>Last week, USDA reported the first U.S. wheat sale to Iraq since the 1989/90 marketing year. The sales come under a United Nations-monitored oil-for-food deal designed to ease the effect of trade sanctions imposed on Iraq for its 1990 invasion of Kuwait.</p>
<p>Iraq was a major customer of U.S. wheat before the trade embargo, ranking number eight in 1989/90 with purchases of 1.3 million tonnes.</p>
<p>Analysts say Iraq increased wheat production by about 50 percent during 1990's but predict the country would still be big customer should the trade sanctions be lifted.</p>
</text>
